WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:03.210
In Hollywood, timing can be everything.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:09.330
Leave a hit show too early, and you risk being
seen as ungrateful or disloyal—but stick

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.710
around too long, and you could end up being
typecast forever.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:19.270
These actors saw high ratings and pursued
their biggest showbiz dreams, only to discover

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.230
just how fickle the spotlight can truly be.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.230
Mischa Barton

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:29.640
Early in her acting career, Mischa Barton
was best known as the puking ghost in The

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:31.000
Sixth Sense.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:36.630
But becoming Marissa Cooper for the teen dramedy
series The O.C. made her a household name.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:41.500
By the time the show's third season started
filming, Barton decided she'd had enough fun

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:46.350
in the sun and ended her contract, which meant
her character was killed off the show.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:50.740
"I was, like, very much ready for closure
on the character and on the series."

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:55.970
Unfortunately for Barton, she later became
a regular in box office bombs, direct-to-video

00:00:55.970 --> 00:01:00.079
films, mugshots, and small TV cameos.

00:01:00.079 --> 00:01:03.170
She was even voted off Dancing With The Stars.

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:04.500
Moral of the story?

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:09.860
Don't look a gift horse — especially one
as cute as Captain Oats — in the mouth.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.340
Katherine Heigl

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:17.560
Her run as Dr. Izzie Stevens on Grey's Anatomy
put Katherine Heigl on top of the A-list.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:21.340
But that was before she publicly dissed and
dismissed the series.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:26.450
In 2008, she withdrew herself from the year's
Emmy nominations and told the press that she

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.980
didn't deserve to be considered because the
season's writing material was unworthy.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:32.920
"Big mistake.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:33.930
Big.

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:34.930
Huge."

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:40.049
She ended up leaving the series in Season
6 and tried her best to become a big screen

00:01:40.049 --> 00:01:43.710
siren with films like Knocked Up and 27 Dresses.

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:48.700
But box office bombs like One for the Money
and The Ugly Truth proved that she wasn't

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.430
as hot to trot as she obviously thought.

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:56.579
Even her attempt to jump back into television
failed to impress, as her 2015 show State

00:01:56.579 --> 00:01:59.720
of Affairs was cancelled after just one season.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:05.120
She probably should've abided by the doctor
rule to "do no harm" to her own career, huh?

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:07.180
David Caruso

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:13.410
After he bailed on NYPD Blue after just one
season in 1994, actor David Caruso pursued

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:18.909
a movie career — which might have been great
if he'd actually picked good movies.

00:02:18.909 --> 00:02:25.609
Instead, Caruso's star status quickly faded
after he appeared in the twin 1995 bombs Jade

00:02:25.609 --> 00:02:27.659
and the aptly named Kiss Of Death.

00:02:27.659 --> 00:02:30.020
“Oh, I’ll give you a kiss alright.

00:02:30.020 --> 00:02:32.319
A kiss of death!”

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:38.040
After enduring a dry spell, he returned to
TV and rebounded with a starring role in CSI:

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:39.040
Miami.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:41.049
Sherry Stringfield

00:02:41.049 --> 00:02:45.969
In the '90s, one of TV's top actresses was
Sherry Stringfield.

00:02:45.969 --> 00:02:52.079
In 1993, she landed a role on the critically
acclaimed cop drama NYPD Blue, and she followed

00:02:52.079 --> 00:02:54.090
David Caruso's own exit.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:58.980
But instead of heading for the movies, Stringfield
joined the hit medical drama ER.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:03.489
After playing Dr. Susan Lewis on the show
for a little over two seasons, she walked

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:07.739
away from that show, too, due to complaints
about the rigorous filming schedule.

00:03:07.739 --> 00:03:12.180
Leaving ER was difficult, though, and she
had to sign an agreement barring herself from

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.730
working on TV for years to get out of her
contract.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:21.019
Stringfield found work in New York teaching
acting classes, later returned to ER for a

00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:26.700
spell, and starred in a few made-for-TV movies,
but she certainly hasn't had the same high-profile

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:29.650
career as some of the series' other stars.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:31.359
Topher Grace

00:03:31.359 --> 00:03:36.810
After seven seasons of playing the lead role
of Eric Forman in That '70s Show, Topher Grace

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:42.099
stepped away from the hit sitcom in 2005 to
pursue other options.

00:03:42.099 --> 00:03:43.540
"Hahaha!"

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.989
At the time, it might've seemed like a good
idea because he'd scored a few leading film

00:03:47.989 --> 00:03:48.989
roles.

00:03:48.989 --> 00:03:52.389
But after he left, Grace had trouble finding
his footing.

00:03:52.389 --> 00:03:57.349
Even though he snagged roles in Spider-Man
3 and other films, he hasn't exactly enjoyed

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:01.509
post-70s Show fame the way some of his other
co-stars have.

00:04:01.509 --> 00:04:02.900
“Right?”

00:04:03.449 --> 00:04:05.120
Lisa Bonet

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:10.299
The Cosby Show was so popular in the late
'80s that Lisa Bonet's character Denise was

00:04:10.299 --> 00:04:15.060
given her own spinoff called A Different World,
which followed her on to college.

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:20.180
The sitcom was a smash, finishing second in
the ratings in its first season — behind

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.729
only The Cosby Show.

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:27.129
But in 1988, Bonet surprisingly left her popular
show after becoming pregnant with her daughter

00:04:27.129 --> 00:04:28.759
Zoe Kravitz.

00:04:28.759 --> 00:04:32.590
Producers on the show were willing to write
her pregnancy into the show, but Bill Cosby

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.099
rejected the idea.

00:04:34.099 --> 00:04:38.790
Rumor has it, Bonet and Cosby weren't on the
greatest terms after she starred in the sexually

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:44.190
explicit movie Angel Heart and did a topless
photoshoot for Interview Magazine — so she

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.190
walked away.

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:49.620
Bonet has only worked sporadically afterward,
and has never quite been the marquee name

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.409
she once was.

00:04:51.409 --> 00:04:53.259
Michael Moriarty

00:04:53.259 --> 00:04:59.169
After then-U.S. Attorney Janet Reno met with
him and producer Dick Wolf in an effort to

00:04:59.169 --> 00:05:04.900
limit television violence, actor Michael Moriarty
quit Law &amp; Order in 1994.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:09.919
Moriarty felt that Reno's efforts were nothing
short of censorship and, when his co-stars

00:05:09.919 --> 00:05:15.669
refused to join his anti-legislation crusade,
he left the series and moved to Canada in

00:05:15.669 --> 00:05:16.669
protest.

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:22.030
He has been able to snag some work as a Canuck
— including a role in Courage Under Fire

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:27.620
and some made-for-TV movies, but he says the
big jobs are off-limits to him now because

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:30.889
he's been blacklisted for speaking out against
the government.

00:05:30.889 --> 00:05:32.180
Joe Piscopo

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:38.979
New Jersey native Joe Piscopo joined the cast
of Saturday Night Live in 1980 and became

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:42.490
one of its biggest stars thanks to his uncanny
celebrity impersonations.

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:44.880
“Should Frank sing a song?

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:46.789
What do you think?”

00:05:46.789 --> 00:05:50.880
“Saw her dancing there, by the record machine.

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:57.240
She could not have been more than 17.”

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:04.020
Piscopo enjoyed major success at SNL but quit
in 1984 alongside co-star Eddie Murphy, who

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:06.280
went on to flourish in the film industry.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:08.840
Piscopo, however, had no such luck.

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:13.620
After a major role in the cult classic Johnny
Dangerously, Piscopo appeared in forgettable

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:18.370
films such as Wise Guys, The House of God,
and Huck and the King of Hearts.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:23.610
Piscopo also followed in Murphy's footsteps
by releasing a music album and touring as

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:28.650
a stand-up comedian, but he hardly enjoyed
the same good fortune as his former co-star.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:34.969
He only really returned to headlines when
he stepped into the political arena in 2016.

00:06:34.969 --> 00:06:36.659
Jeff Conaway

00:06:36.659 --> 00:06:41.930
You might remember Jeff Conaway as the greased
lightning ringleader Kenickie in Grease, but

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:45.220
he also enjoyed success on the small screen
with Taxi.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:50.590
In what would prove to be a sadly ironic turn,
Conaway played struggling actor Bobby Wheeler

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:55.990
and left the show in 1981 partly because he
thought his character made actors look bad.

00:06:55.990 --> 00:07:01.360
In the years after he left Taxi, Conaway couldn't
land a single movie role and his fantasy TV

00:07:01.360 --> 00:07:06.110
series Wizards and Warriors was canceled after
just three episodes.

00:07:06.110 --> 00:07:10.410
Conaway's career went downhill after that
and the actor, who struggled with addiction,

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:13.750
died in 2011 at the age of 60.

00:07:13.750 --> 00:07:15.990
McLean Stevenson

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:22.000
After playing Lieutenant Colonel Henry Blake
for two seasons, McLean Stevenson quit M*A*S*H

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:27.729
in 1975, winning a Golden Globe and an Emmy
nomination for his efforts.

00:07:27.729 --> 00:07:33.870
Lured by the promise of bigger paydays, Stevenson
left to pursue starring ventures outside the

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:38.180
show's ensemble cast…but it didn't work
out so well.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:43.580
His next four sitcoms, including The McLean
Stevenson Show, fizzled out and were generally

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:45.530
panned by critics.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:50.520
Stevenson later admitted he regretted leaving
M*A*S*H, telling the Los Angeles Times in

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:56.199
1991: "The mistake was that I thought everybody
in America loved McLean Stevenson.

00:07:56.199 --> 00:07:57.860
That was not the case.

00:07:57.860 --> 00:08:00.069
Everybody loved Henry Blake."

00:08:00.069 --> 00:08:01.069
Thanks for watching!

00:08:01.069 --> 00:08:04.039
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:04.039 --> 00:08:07.400
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

